<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="scim"
      updated="August 23, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"enable", "Enabling directory sync"},
        {"protocol", "SCIM protocol reference"},
        {"providers", "Provider guides"},
        {"role-mapping", "Role mapping"},
        {"runner-access-mapping", "Mapping groups to runners"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="scim">
        <:dek>
          Directory sync uses SCIM 2.0 to let your identity provider manage members in emisar:
          it provisions people, and when they leave it suspends the member, ends their
          sessions, and revokes their API keys.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Set up SSO login first">
        <p>
          Directory sync builds on a configured SSO connection. If you have not connected
          your identity provider, start with <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Single sign-on</.link>. Then enable directory sync on that connection.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="enable">Enabling directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open the configured connection from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/sso"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team → Single sign-on</a>
        and enable <strong class="text-zinc-200">directory sync</strong>
        — the panel lives on the connection's page, under its settings.
        <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-directory-sync.webp"
        title="emisar console · Directory sync (SCIM)"
        caption="A configured connection: the SCIM base URL to copy, token rotation, and the three steps for pointing your IdP at it."
        alt="The Directory sync (SCIM) panel on a configured connection: an enabled badge, Rotate token and Disable buttons, the SCIM base URL with a copy button, and the expanded 'Point your IdP at this connection' guide — enable SCIM provisioning at your provider, set that base URL as the connector's endpoint and paste the bearer token as its API token, and map the SCIM externalId to the same value the OIDC sub claim carries."
      />

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              From <a
                href={~p"/app/sso"}
                class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
              >Team → Single sign-on</a>, open the connection and click <span class="text-zinc-300">Enable directory sync</span>.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              emisar shows you two things to wire into your provider's SCIM connector:
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  the <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM base URL</strong>
                  —
                  <.docs_inline_code>/scim/v2</.docs_inline_code>
                  on your emisar host (copy button beside it)
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  the <strong class="text-zinc-200">bearer token</strong>
                  — shown once when you enable or rotate, so copy it immediately; if you
                  missed it, <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotate token</strong>
                  mints a new one and the old one stops working.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Point your IdP's SCIM connector at that base URL and bearer.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Set the SCIM endpoint to the base URL, paste the token as the API token (emisar
              reads it from the
              <.docs_inline_code>Authorization</.docs_inline_code>
              header), and enable Create, Update, and Deactivate. Okta and Entra need a
              separate provisioning application — their OIDC login applications cannot
              provision through SCIM — while JumpCloud carries sign-in and provisioning in
              one. The <strong class="text-zinc-200">“Point your IdP at this connection”</strong>
              guide lists the provider-specific settings.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Match the SCIM externalId to the OIDC subject.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Configure
              <.docs_inline_code>externalId</.docs_inline_code>
              to carry the same stable value as the connection's identifier claim, so the SSO
              login and the synced record resolve to one emisar identity. Matching never uses
              email, so identifiers that differ cannot be reconciled.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Okta</strong>
                  usually needs no changes: it puts the Okta user ID in <.docs_inline_code>externalId</.docs_inline_code>, and
                  its organization authorization server sends the same value as
                  <.docs_inline_code>sub</.docs_inline_code>
                  — though Okta shows that in examples rather than guaranteeing it, and a
                  custom authorization server can rewrite the subject claim.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Entra</strong>
                  needs changes on both sides: map
                  <.docs_inline_code>externalId</.docs_inline_code>
                  from
                  <.docs_inline_code>mailNickname</.docs_inline_code>
                  to <.docs_inline_code>objectId</.docs_inline_code>, and
                  set the connection's identifier claim to <.docs_inline_code>oid</.docs_inline_code>. Entra sends
                  a different
                  <.docs_inline_code>sub</.docs_inline_code>
                  per application, so without both changes the login and provisioning
                  applications identify different people.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-2">
              Whatever the provider, check both values against one real user before you roll out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning</strong>
            creates the user and membership at the connection's default role before their
            first login, with runner access combined from Default runner access and mapped
            directory groups.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            When the directory deactivates or removes a user,
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">deprovisioning suspends the member</strong>
            — sessions end and their API keys are revoked immediately, so directory
            offboarding revokes emisar access on its own. The person and audit history remain.
            Deactivation can be reversed with <code>active:true</code>; after DELETE, the
            directory must POST the same <code>externalId</code>
            to restore the resource.
            emisar refuses to deprovision the
            last active owner and returns an error to the directory — promote another owner
            first.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="disable-sync">Rotate or disable directory sync</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotate token</strong>
        mints a new bearer and the old one stops working immediately — paste the new value
        into your IdP connector. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Disable</strong>
        also stops the bearer, but members are kept: the directory just stops managing their
        role and runner access, and owners and admins control those again. If you re-enable
        sync later, group-based access returns only after the directory pushes its groups
        again.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Because there is no overlap, the connector is unauthenticated between the rotate and the
        paste — you should plan for that gap. The rotation sequence, and how a SCIM
        bearer compares with every other credential emisar issues, is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >rotate and revoke credentials</.link>. If you believe the bearer has leaked, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security incidents</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-synced-users.webp"
        title="emisar console · Synced members"
        caption="Once your IdP is connected, provisioned members appear here with the directory id they came from and the role their groups earned them."
        alt="The Synced members list on a connection: two members provisioned over SCIM, each with a SCIM badge, the directory externalId they were provisioned under, when they last synced, a locked role chip showing the role their group mapping earned them, and a Suspend action."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="protocol">SCIM protocol reference</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every endpoint is under the connection's <code>/scim/v2</code> base URL and requires its
        bearer token. The token selects the account and provider. Resources cannot be read or
        changed across connections.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Resource</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Supported operations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-100">
                /ServiceProviderConfig<br />/ResourceTypes<br />/Schemas
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Authenticated <code>GET</code> discovery, including individual ResourceType and
                Schema resources for User and Group.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-100">/Users</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Collections support <code>GET</code>
                and <code>POST</code>. Resources support <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, and <code>DELETE</code>.
                Resource paths use the immutable <code>id</code>
                returned by emisar.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-100">/Groups</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Collections support <code>GET</code>
                and <code>POST</code>. Resources support <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, and <code>DELETE</code>.
                Resource paths use the returned <code>id</code>. Group writes reconcile the pushed
                membership before mappings are applied.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h3 id="user-contract">Users</.docs_h3>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <code>POST</code>
            requires <code>externalId</code>
            or <code>userName</code>. <code>active</code>
            defaults to true. emisar accepts a JSON boolean or the case-insensitive strings <code>"True"</code>/<code>"False"</code>
            that Entra sends. Repeating a create with the same <code>externalId</code>
            reconciles the existing identity and returns the same server <code>id</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Collection filters support <code>userName eq "..."</code>
            and <code>externalId eq "..."</code>. Unsupported filters return <code>invalidFilter</code>.
            Collections use one-based <code>startIndex</code>/<code>count</code>
            pagination, return at most 100 resources per page, and report the full matching count
            in <code>totalResults</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <code>PATCH</code>
            accepts <code>add</code>
            or <code>replace</code>
            for <code>active</code>, <code>displayName</code>, <code>name.givenName</code>, and <code>name.familyName</code>, including the pathless attribute map Entra sends.
            <code>PUT</code>
            requires <code>active</code>
            in one of those boolean forms and can update the name.
            Email and <code>externalId</code>
            stay fixed after provisioning.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <code>PATCH active:false</code>
            suspends the membership while keeping the User resource addressable, and
            <code>active:true</code>
            restores that suspension. <code>DELETE</code>
            also retires the User resource: later reads and mutations return 404 until the
            directory POSTs the same <code>externalId</code>.
            A manual administrator
            suspension is not reversed by the directory.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="group-contract">Groups</.docs_h3>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <code>POST</code>
            and <code>PUT</code>
            reconcile the full membership. <code>PATCH</code>
            supports member <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code>, and whole-set <code>replace</code>, plus
            <code>displayName</code>
            replacement. Each <code>members[].value</code>
            is the returned <code>id</code>
            of a User resource.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            A Group <code>POST</code>
            with <code>externalId</code>
            reconciles that directory resource. A probe without <code>externalId</code>
            gets a new
            server <code>id</code>. Use that id for later reads and writes.
            <code>displayName</code>
            is a label and filter, never the group's identity.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Collection filtering supports <code>displayName eq "..."</code>
            and uses the same <code>startIndex</code>/<code>count</code>
            pagination as Users. Deleting a group
            removes everyone from it, recomputes their mapped authorization, then retires the SCIM
            group resource.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            One request accepts at most 100 PATCH operations. A group write accepts at most 5,000
            member IDs. Bulk, sorting, ETags, and password changes are not supported.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider guides</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The IdP-side walkthrough lives on each provider's guide, beside its sign-in setup:
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li id="okta" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Okta</.link>
            — SCIM rides its own Okta app, separate from the sign-in integration.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="entra" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Microsoft Entra</.link>
            — a separate enterprise application, with
            <.docs_inline_code>externalId</.docs_inline_code>
            remapped to <.docs_inline_code>objectId</.docs_inline_code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="jumpcloud" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >JumpCloud</.link>
            — the same custom application that signs people in.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="keycloak" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Keycloak</.link>
            has no outbound SCIM. The guide explains the remaining manual work.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="google-workspace" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace#scim"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Google Workspace</.link>
            has no outbound SCIM. Route it through an IdP to close the
            offboarding gap.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="role-mapping">Role mapping</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Role mapping pairs an exact synced directory group with an emisar role
        — <strong class="text-zinc-200">viewer</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">operator</strong>, <strong class="text-zinc-200">billing manager</strong>,
        or <strong class="text-zinc-200">admin</strong>. A member in several mapped groups gets the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">highest</strong>
        role mapped to any of them. Renaming a group does not break its mapping.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Owner is never assignable through
        sync</strong>
        — it stays a deliberate human assignment.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/scim-group-role-mapping.webp"
        title="emisar console · Role mapping"
        caption="Each mapping pairs one exact synced directory group with the role its members land at. A synced group you don't map leaves its members at the connection's default role."
        alt="The Role mapping panel on a connection: two mappings — the Platform Engineers directory group (externalId kc-grp-platform) mapped to Admin, and SRE On-call (kc-grp-sre) mapped to Operator — each with Edit and Delete, plus an Add mapping button."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-access-mapping">Mapping directory groups to runner access</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Map a synced directory group to All runners, selected runner groups, or individual
        runners. The mapping applies to current group
        members immediately and follows later SCIM membership changes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Group runner access is <strong class="text-zinc-200">additive</strong>: emisar
        combines Default runner access with every mapped group the member belongs to, a
        mapping cannot remove access granted elsewhere, and any All runners grant wins. For example, a no-runners default plus
        <code>database-team → group:database-prod</code>
        gives those members exactly the database runner group — and if another mapped group
        grants all runners, members in both get all runners.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Wait for the first group push">
        <p>
          emisar does not treat "no groups received yet" as "this member belongs to no groups."
          Until the directory sends its first group snapshot, members keep the authorization they
          already had. After that snapshot, removing the last mapped group returns the role to the
          connection default and removes that group's runner grant.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use these checks for directory-side symptoms. If you are not sure the problem is
        provisioning, start from <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Troubleshooting</.link>. It routes each symptom to the page that owns it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Symptom</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                The IdP cannot verify credentials
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the connection's exact SCIM base URL and send the token as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>. If the token was rotated or sync was
                disabled, the old value is invalid immediately.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                The connection says Never synced
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The bearer has not authenticated successfully yet. Run the IdP connector's test,
                then check that provisioning is enabled and assigned. After the first request,
                emisar shows <strong class="text-zinc-200">Last sync</strong> with its age.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                SSO login and SCIM provisioning do not converge
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Compare one user's SCIM <code>externalId</code>
                with the OIDC identifier claim.
                They must be the same stable value. For Entra, map <code>externalId</code>
                to <code>objectId</code>
                and use <code>oid</code>
                for OIDC.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                A group mapping has no effect
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Confirm the group appears under Synced groups, its SCIM <code>externalId</code>
                exactly matches the mapping, and the IdP pushed members. A display name is not the
                mapping key.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Offboarding returns a conflict
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                emisar refuses to suspend or delete the account's last active owner. Promote
                another owner, then retry the directory operation.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            The
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SCIM bearer token is an admin-grade credential</strong>
            — it can provision and deprovision across the account, so treat it like a
            password and rotate it from
            <a
              href={~p"/app/sso"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Team → Single sign-on</a>
            whenever it is exposed; your IdP loses access until you paste the new token.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Each directory-sync action records its authentication method in the <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >
              audit log
            </.link>. This shows whether the directory, rather than a person, suspended an offboarded
            member.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
